WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.696 --> 00:00:16.942
When got home from school, she ran straight to the mailbox.

2
00:00:18.003 --> 00:00:24.345
Grandpa had gone to the Southwest a week ago but hadn't sent her anything yet.

3
00:00:25.406 --> 00:00:26.706
Had he forgotten about her?

4
00:00:27.787 --> 00:00:31.008
She saw a postcard and a yellow envelope addressed to her.

5
00:00:31.952 --> 00:00:33.593
"Finally!" she thought.

6
00:00:34.713 --> 00:00:35.573
Hi, Laurie!

7
00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:38.334
How's my favorite granddaughter?

8
00:00:39.394 --> 00:00:40.694
I made it to New Mexico.

9
00:00:41.995 --> 00:00:50.597
I'm in Chaco Culture National Historical Park studying the ancient Pueblos— Native Americans who lived in the Southwest

10
00:00:51.037 --> 00:00:52.918
over 1,500 years ago.

11
00:00:54.178 --> 00:01:00.560
Many different peoples lived here, but as a group, we call them the Pueblos, after the houses they

12
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:06.555
built. Their houses had connecting walls and rooms built on top of one another.

13
00:01:07.595 --> 00:01:10.997
They were really more like villages than individual houses.

14
00:01:12.618 --> 00:01:16.640
The biggest Pueblo settlements are found here in Chaco Canyon.

15
00:01:17.821 --> 00:01:20.943
We archaeologists call them the great houses.

16
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:30.088
One house, Pueblo Bonito, covered about three acres, and probably housed about twelve hundred people.

17
00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:37.145
Pueblo society had no leaders, so living together took a lot of organization and patience.

18
00:01:38.226 --> 00:01:43.250
But amazingly, they were one of the most peaceful people this country has ever known.

19
00:01:44.911 --> 00:01:48.654
I'll write you a letter explaining more when I set up camp tonight.

20
00:01:49.655 --> 00:01:53.898
But I had to send you a postcard of the amazing Pueblo Bonito!

21
00:01:54.959 --> 00:01:57.281
Laurie examined the image on the postcard.

22
00:01:58.422 --> 00:02:00.424
All the walls really were connected!

23
00:02:01.552 --> 00:02:05.595
She tried to imagine living in a house that was connected to everybody else's.

24
00:02:06.655 --> 00:02:10.318
It would be strange to live so close to her classmates and teacher!

25
00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:15.020
She wished she could have gone with Grandpa to see the ancient sites.

26
00:02:16.101 --> 00:02:18.503
There were so many things she wanted to know.

27
00:02:19.663 --> 00:02:24.967
If the Pueblos moved into the Southwest 1,500 years ago, where were they before that?

28
00:02:26.147 --> 00:02:26.968
Where were they now?

29
00:02:29.012 --> 00:02:31.973
She opened the envelope and started reading Grandpa's letter.

30
00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:37.014
Dear Laurie, I know you wish you were here and I do too.

31
00:02:38.175 --> 00:02:41.396
I bet you also have lots of questions about the Pueblos.

32
00:02:43.116 --> 00:02:44.857
So let's start at the beginning.

33
00:02:46.097 --> 00:02:52.459
Native Americans had been living in the United States for thousands of years before the Europeans arrived and

34
00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:53.839
colonized the land.

35
00:02:55.020 --> 00:03:02.085
Many historians think the natives originally came from Asia and arrived in North America during the Ice Age.

36
00:03:03.186 --> 00:03:07.249
Back then a land bridge connected Asia to Alaska.

37
00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:15.496
Native Americans might have crossed this land bridge while following bison and other large mammals they hunted.

38
00:03:16.597 --> 00:03:19.839
But Pueblos have their own version of how they got here.

39
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:26.992
They believe they came from the earth, in a holy place underground, and were then led to the

40
00:03:27.072 --> 00:03:28.253
surface by a god.

41
00:03:29.513 --> 00:03:35.875
Origins aside, we know for sure that the first Pueblos were nomadic hunters and gatherers.

42
00:03:37.075 --> 00:03:39.796
Eventually they began building pit houses.

43
00:03:42.557 --> 00:03:47.658
These were circular underground houses, dug five or six feet into the ground.

44
00:03:48.819 --> 00:03:56.826
Around the year 700, they began building aboveground, but kept underground rooms for special religious ceremonies.

45
00:03:58.006 --> 00:04:04.028
Now their houses were made of stone and red-clay bricks, but still had no doors or windows.

46
00:04:05.189 --> 00:04:08.370
So how did the Pueblos get in and out of their houses?

47
00:04:10.831 --> 00:04:11.591
Through the roof!

48
00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:18.114
They climbed up a wooden ladder to the top of the wall, and then down another ladder to

49
00:04:18.194 --> 00:04:19.034
enter their homes.

50
00:04:20.235 --> 00:04:25.617
Imagine you're returning home after a hunt, carrying a large deer over your back.

51
00:04:26.657 --> 00:04:31.039
Or you're coming back from the spring with two large jugs full of water!

52
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.281
You would become a very skilled ladder climber!

53
00:04:36.862 --> 00:04:41.023
Maybe you'd like to know a little more about the Pueblos' daily life.

54
00:04:42.204 --> 00:04:43.324
They worked very hard.

55
00:04:44.505 --> 00:04:49.067
Women ground corn and gathered seeds, berries, and nuts for the family meals.

56
00:04:50.207 --> 00:04:51.588
They also made pottery.

57
00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:56.815
Men took care of the crops, went on hunts, and wove baskets and blankets.

58
00:04:57.975 --> 00:05:00.677
Young children and girls stayed home to help their mothers.

59
00:05:01.777 --> 00:05:07.140
But older boys followed the men, learning everything they would need to know to become adults in the

60
00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:13.924
village. It was hot during the daytime, when the plateaus were lit by the sun, but nights were

61
00:05:13.964 --> 00:05:21.068
cold. The Pueblos ate warm cornbread, beans, and squash, and roasted meat over fires.

62
00:05:22.272 --> 00:05:28.734
Then they curled up in rabbit-skin robes and turkey-feather blankets and went to sleep because at sunrise they

63
00:05:28.754 --> 00:05:30.335
would start another long day.

64
00:05:31.495 --> 00:05:34.896
But guess what these hardworking people did in their free time, Laurie?

65
00:05:38.077 --> 00:05:39.257
They played games!

66
00:05:40.097 --> 00:05:45.139
Running races, dart-throwing contests, and ball games were all popular.

67
00:05:46.219 --> 00:05:52.663
Of course the games were fun, but Pueblos also believed that these games were invented by the gods.

68
00:05:53.784 --> 00:06:00.369
So playing them pleased the gods and helped keep rivers flowing, rain falling, and the sun shining in

69
00:06:00.409 --> 00:06:00.949
the sky.

70
00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:07.333
When I get back, let's see if playing kickball every day will keep the gods happy and bring

71
00:06:07.393 --> 00:06:09.975
us lots of good sledding snow this winter!

72
00:06:11.416 --> 00:06:13.698
Missing you, Grandpa P.S.

73
00:06:14.198 --> 00:06:16.560
I'll send you an email from the next site.

74
00:06:23.968 --> 00:06:26.630
Laurie ran upstairs to the computer in the study.

75
00:06:33.173 --> 00:06:35.695
In her inbox was an e-card from Grandpa.

76
00:06:37.996 --> 00:06:41.839
Laurie—I'm in Mesa Verde National Park in Colorado.

77
00:06:42.779 --> 00:06:48.603
Here Pueblos farmed, built houses, wove baskets, and performed rain dances.

78
00:06:49.683 --> 00:06:55.803
But in the 1200s, they moved down into the cliffs and built their homes into the steep walls

79
00:06:55.863 --> 00:06:58.224
of canyons, perhaps for protection.

80
00:06:59.265 --> 00:07:04.628
These peaceful people spent their time making pottery and offering respect to the gods.

81
00:07:05.809 --> 00:07:11.052
Then, about a hundred years later, they mysteriously left Mesa Verde forever.

82
00:07:12.573 --> 00:07:16.175
Were they escaping something or had they simply outgrown their homes?

83
00:07:17.395 --> 00:07:18.116
No one knows.

84
00:07:19.244 --> 00:07:26.388
They continued to live nearby until the Spanish arrived in the 1500s and forced the Pueblos to leave

85
00:07:26.448 --> 00:07:27.589
their homes yet again.

86
00:07:28.649 --> 00:07:32.111
But Pueblo ancestors still live in the Southwest.

87
00:07:33.212 --> 00:07:37.274
In fact, I'll meet some modern Pueblos tomorrow at a ceremony!

88
00:07:38.315 --> 00:07:38.955
See you soon!

89
00:07:38.975 --> 00:07:44.118
–Grandpa Laurie looked at the photograph on the e-card.

90
00:07:45.298 --> 00:07:50.902
She couldn't imagine climbing in and out of that big canyon every day just to get home.

91
00:07:52.022 --> 00:07:54.804
The Pueblos must have been so strong!

92
00:07:55.984 --> 00:07:57.485
Laurie heard the front door open.

93
00:07:57.505 --> 00:08:00.207
"Laurie! Are you home?

94
00:08:01.167 --> 00:08:02.068
Sorry I'm late!"

95
00:08:02.428 --> 00:08:03.089
Mom called.

96
00:08:03.109 --> 00:08:05.130
"I'm up here, Mom!

97
00:08:06.591 --> 00:08:08.812
I got mail from Grandpa in New Mexico!"
